C = [
("h1", "1. Two Men, Twice"),
("p", "Claim: Paul does not set Adam and Christ side by side by habit. He does it twice, and each time the pairing is doing a different job. A reader who takes the two passages for one teaching repeated has already decided the question this volume asks."),
("sub", "1.1 The two places"),
("p", "The first place is Romans 5:12–21. It follows a long argument about how a man is made right with God, and it opens with a word that looks backward: “Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned” (Romans 5:12). The sentence begins a comparison and does not finish it. Paul breaks off in verse 13 to explain how sin stood in the world before the law, and he takes up the comparison again at verse 18."),
("p", "The second place is 1 Corinthians 15:21–22, with a longer return at 15:45–49. It stands in a chapter about the resurrection of the dead. “For since by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead” (1 Corinthians 15:21). “For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive” (1 Corinthians 15:22). Twenty-three verses later the pairing comes back with different names: “The first man Adam was made a living soul; the last Adam was made a quickening spirit” (1 Corinthians 15:45)."),
("sub", "1.2 The names Paul uses"),
("p", "Paul does not call the two men by the same names in the two places. The table lists what he calls them. The difference in names is the first sign that the two passages are not saying the same thing."),
("ctbl", "How each passage names the two men.", [
    ("Passage", "The first man", "The second man"),
    ("Romans 5:12–14", "“one man” (5:12); “Adam” (5:14)", "“him that was to come” (5:14)"),
    ("Romans 5:15–19", "“one” and “one man’s offence” and “one that sinned” and “one man’s disobedience” (5:15–19)", "“one man, Jesus Christ” (5:15); “one, Jesus Christ” (5:17); “the righteousness of one” (5:18); “the obedience of one” (5:19)"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15:21–22", "“man” (15:21); “Adam” (15:22)", "“man” (15:21); “Christ” (15:22)"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15:45–49", "“The first man Adam”; “the first man”; “the earthy”", "“the last Adam”; “the second man”; “the Lord from heaven”; “the heavenly”"),
  ], [0.22, 0.39, 0.39]),
("p", "Romans 5 never calls Adam “the first man,” and it never calls Christ “the last Adam” or “the second man.” First Corinthians 15 does not use the words “offence,” “disobedience” or “obedience” of either man. The vocabulary of Romans 5 is the vocabulary of an act: an offence, a gift, a judgment, an obedience. The vocabulary of 1 Corinthians 15 is the vocabulary of a body: natural and spiritual, earthy and heavenly, sown and raised. Both vocabularies are Paul’s, and both are about two men. They are not the same vocabulary."),
("sub", "1.3 How the two men are joined to the rest"),
("p", "A second difference lies in the small words that join each man to those who come after him. Romans 5 uses “by.” Sin entered “by one man,” judgment came “by the offence of one,” and the free gift came “by the righteousness of one” (Romans 5:12, 18). The words “in Adam” do not occur in Romans 5:12–21. First Corinthians 15 uses “in.” “As in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive” (1 Corinthians 15:22)."),
("p", "The difference should not be pressed past what it shows. “By” says how a result reached the many: through one man’s act. “In” says where the many are found: in one man’s company, under his headship. Romans 5 is about the first, and the second is added in 1 Corinthians 15. The two ideas fit together, and a reader may hold both. What a reader may not do is read the “in” of 1 Corinthians 15 back into every “by” of Romans 5, as though Paul had written “in Adam” where he wrote “by one man.” He wrote what he wrote, and the volume reads it."),
("sub", "1.4 What a head does"),
("p", "In both passages one man stands for many. That is what is meant here by a head. A head is a man whose act, or whose life, has results for others who are joined to him. Paul does not define the word in either passage, and the volume does not impose a definition on him. It reports what each passage says the head does, and for whom."),
("tbl", "What the first man and the second man are said to do, in the order of the verses.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:12", "“by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin”", "The first man’s act, and its two results: sin and death"),
    ("Rom 5:18", "“by the offence of one judgment came upon all men to condemnation”", "One act, one result: judgment unto condemnation"),
    ("Rom 5:18", "“by the righteousness of one the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life”", "The second man’s act, and its result: the free gift"),
    ("1Cor 15:21", "“by man came death, by man came also the resurrection of the dead”", "Death and resurrection set against each other"),
    ("1Cor 15:22", "“in Adam all die, even so in Christ shall all be made alive”", "A single sentence with both men and both results"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "1.5 How the volume reads"),
("p", "The volume asks of each clause one question: what is this clause answering? In Romans 5 the question behind the passage is how sin, death and righteousness reached “all men.” In 1 Corinthians 15 the question is the one put by the man who “will say, How are the dead raised up? and with what body do they come?” (1 Corinthians 15:35). The two questions are different. When they are kept apart, most of what is difficult in the two passages falls into place, and what remains is argued openly in the chapters that follow."),
("p", "The order of the chapters follows the order of the letters. Chapters 2 to 7 read Romans 5. Chapters 8 to 12 read 1 Corinthians 15. Chapter 13 takes the clauses of both passages one at a time. Chapters 14 and 15 set the two side by side, and say what neither passage teaches. Chapter 16 gives the objections."),
]
